#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d5c96d-ca13-5c1e-83d3-0dc9f6da0f83" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Vectors are Parallel"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096649 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc49f831-8e1d-5fb4-8952-1dd4ee3bfa02" :knowledge-point/title "Creating Parallel Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc5bab6c-1ce7-58b5-a59d-cff2ce35ecca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parallel Vectors From a Diagram"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097832 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ shown in the picture below. The vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$ and a length (magnitude) that is twice the length of $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n|2\\mathbf{a}|=2 \\cdot |\\mathbf{a}|\n$$\n\n![](images/01/01c393b2df3d39e4f62faca475a26ea835e1ab88266628509d58e21edd36c79b.png)\n\nOn the other hand, $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$ and its magnitude is $1.5$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$: \n\n$$\n|-1.5\\mathbf{a}|=1.5 \\cdot |\\mathbf{a}|\n$$\n\n![](images/73/73ea353af74ab9518ea7a963985ad26ad36723c7e3a335391628f1eba208139e.png)\n\nNotice that $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ are both parallel to $\\mathbf a,$ and are parallel to each other." :tutorial/id #uuid "977eec28-841a-556b-a6c4-937a625db1b3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2905 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097834 :tutorial/content "For any vector $\\mathbf{a}$ the vector $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ is equal to the zero vector: \n\n$$\n0\\cdot\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to *any* vector." :tutorial/id #uuid "d5934891-d63d-5d05-be3e-b864a8081187" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2907 :tutorial/title "The Zero Vector is Parallel to Any Vector"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097852 :tutorial/content "In general, two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are called **parallel** (or **collinear**) if there exists a number $\\lambda$ such that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\lambda \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSometimes, we'll use a special notation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\parallel \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nto denote that $\\mathbf{a}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "9fc7b4c2-c418-5980-88be-ea7609a45ba0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3401 :tutorial/title "Determining Whether Two Vectors Are Parallel"}]}
